{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcHCofJ1ctfzHTyASHLo", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Dana, yes. Production changed after you called the freeze. I didn't do it, and I didn't stop it.\n\n**What changed** (I compared this morning's read-only checks with what I saw last night):\n- **13 customers were deleted.** The dashboard now says 201. Last night it said 214.\n- Those 13 are the 12 `@acme-internal.test` test accounts **plus Testa Analytics** (id 74, `billing@testa.io`, $799/mo). Testa is the real customer I warned about last night, and it's gone along with all its billing rows.\n- **13 subscriptions and 36 invoices were deleted** with them. MRR on the dashboard dropped from $76,036 to $69,849, and open invoices from 71 to 68.\n- Nothing else looks different. No schema changes, `schema_migrations` is unchanged (still 001 to 003), and nothing was added to the audit log.\n- I can't tell who ran the deletes or exactly when. It was some time after my check last night, which showed 214. The app's audit log doesn't record it. The Postgres statement log would, and ops can pull it.\n- A correction to my message last night: the test accounts had 35 invoices, not 41. I miscounted.\n\n**Can we get back to exactly the freeze state?** Most likely yes. The runbook says ops creates a named restore point when a freeze starts, and WAL archiving is on. Ops would:\n1. restore the latest base backup into a fresh instance,\n2. replay the archive up to the freeze restore point (or a timestamp just before the deletes),\n3. check it shows 214 customers, $76,036 MRR and 71 open invoices, with Testa present,\n4. swap it in.\n\nAny real writes made after the freeze would be lost in that restore. I see no app activity since then, so I don't think anything would be lost. I haven't confirmed the restore point or archive actually exist. Ops needs to check that first. If you only want Testa back before the d